# Everything below is GENERATED by running this project. None of it belongs in
# git — it is either large, machine-specific, or a snapshot of a site that will
# have changed by the time anyone reads it. A single 3-page run drops
# ~5 MB of HTML snapshots alone.

# --- scrape output -------------------------------------------------------
*.json
*.csv
!package.json
# Committed on purpose: a real 5-row excerpt of a real run, so a reader can see
# the exact output shape without installing anything.
!sample_output.json
!sample_output.csv
# The offline suite's fixtures. Generated by make_fixtures.py from
# real captures, verified to parse identically to them and scrubbed;
# it has to be IN the repo or the suite cannot run on a clean clone.
!fixtures_generated.json
dubizzle_listings.*
dubizzle_listings_*.*

# --- the live-test harness's working directory ---------------------------
# `--dump-html PATH` writes PATH.page1, PATH.page2, ... — FILES, not a
# directory. Ignoring only `live_results/` let two 1.5 MB page dumps be
# committed by a merge; each carried 26 per-seller UUIDs, 12 copies of the
# site's Algolia key and its Sentry keys, none of which belongs here. Both
# spellings are ignored now, and the offline suite asserts that no raw
# capture is tracked.
live_results/
live_results.*
# ANY working directory a live run writes into, not just the one name this
# family's older repos used. `live/sel_dump.html` — a 1.5 MB raw page dump —
# was staged by `git add -A` on this repo's very first commit because only
# `live_results/` was listed here. The lesson is the same one a sibling
# already paid for, arriving through a directory nobody had listed, so the
# pattern is now the KIND of directory rather than one chosen name.
live/
out/
output/
run/
runs/
results/
scratch/
*.page[0-9]
*.page[0-9][0-9]
live_results.zip

# --- debug artifacts the engines drop next to themselves -----------------
# Written on purpose when a page yields nothing or comes back blocked, and by
# --dump-html on success too. Large, and stale within hours.
*_debug.html
*_debug.png
*.html.page*

# --- captures ------------------------------------------------------------
# COMMITTED, deliberately, and trimmed to the data blocks the parser reads —
# 18.2 MB of raw responses down to 4.8 MB, with every file verified to parse
# byte-identically to the original it was cut from before it was written
# back (see captures/README.md).
#
# They are committed because `smoke_test.py` pins VALUES against them, and a
# check whose fixture is absent does not fail — it SKIPS, which reads as
# green. A suite that quietly stops testing the parser the moment nobody has
# the captures locally is the failure mode this family has paid for.
#
# What must never be committed is the personal material inside them.
# `scrub_fixtures.py` replaces it and the suite guards the result by pattern.
#
# Anything else a capture run drops beside them, though, stays out:
captures/*.zip
captures/raw/

# --- the landing page and the proxy list ---------------------------------
# Both live outside the repo on purpose: the landing page is published
# elsewhere, and a downloaded 2captcha proxy list is a credential file.
index.html
proxylist.txt

# --- caches --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.fingerprint_cache/

# --- packaging build artifacts --------------------------------------------
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# --- environments --------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- editors / OS --------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- never, under any circumstances --------------------------------------
# The scrapers mask user:pass in their own logs, but raw HTML dumps and the
# Scraper API's x-debug header are NOT masked, and a key pasted into a shell
# history file is not masked either.
*.log
.env
*.key
secrets*

# Any .env variant, however it was renamed. `.env` alone is not enough, and
# this has cost the family twice: craigslist-scraper's own .gitignore records
# a `.env.hold` made while testing that was committed WITH A LIVE KEY in it,
# recoverable only because that repo was private at the time. On 2026-09-17
# a sibling had an untracked `.env.bak` holding a 32-hex key in a PUBLIC
# repository, one `git add -A` from publication. Craigslist was fixed then
# and the fix never reached anyone else, which is §16's rule unapplied: when
# you fix one, check the siblings.
.env*
!.env.example
